// Generated by help-site/build.js
window.HELP_SEARCH = [{"t":"Introduction","p":"Chapter 1","u":"introduction.html","x":""},{"t":"What this system does","p":"Chapter 1: Introduction","u":"introduction.html#what-this-system-does","x":"IBS Haulage is a web-based system for running a haulage or trucking business from one place. It follows a job from the client’s first request through to the money in your account. With it, your team can: Record transport requests from clients, send quotes, and revise them until the client agrees Turn an accepted request into a trip, assign a truck and a driver, and track the trip stage by stage Keep a register of trucks and drivers, see who is free, and get warned before a driver’s licence expires Log trip expenses such as fuel, tolls and allowances, and see each trip’s profit or loss Send proforma invoices (priced documents clients can approve before the work), and turn them into invoices Invoice clients for one or more trips, record payments, and follow what is still owed Manage cash, mobile money and bank accounts, move money between them, and record bank charges Run reports on revenue, receivables, trip profitability, expenses, payments and fleet use Give clients their own portal to send requests, accept quotes, follow trips and download invoices Control who can see and do what, with three staff roles and an audit log of every change"},{"t":"Who this manual is for","p":"Chapter 1: Introduction","u":"introduction.html#who-this-manual-is-for","x":"This manual is for the people who use the system every day: operations staff who handle requests, trips and the fleet; finance staff who handle clients, invoices, payments and accounts; and administrators who set the system up and manage users. Chapter 14 describes the client portal, so you can help clients who use it."},{"t":"How this manual is organized","p":"Chapter 1: Introduction","u":"introduction.html#how-this-manual-is-organized","x":"Chapters follow the order a job moves through the business. Chapters 1 and 2 get you started. Chapters 3 to 6 cover the operations side: requests, trips, the fleet, and the locations and service types they use. Chapters 7 to 11 cover the finance side: clients, invoices, payments, proforma invoices and accounts. Chapter 12 covers reports, Chapter 13 administration, and Chapter 14 the client portal. Appendix A answers common questions."},{"t":"Conventions used in this manual","p":"Chapter 1: Introduction","u":"introduction.html#conventions-used-in-this-manual","x":"Names in quotes, e.g. “Save Quote”, refer to an exact button, field, or menu label in the system. An arrow shows a path through the menu, e.g. Finance → Invoices. Numbered steps are things you do, in order, on screen. A shaded “Note” box calls out something useful to know. A shaded “Caution” box calls out something that cannot be undone or that affects other records. A shaded “Who can do this” box tells you which role a feature needs."},{"t":"Roles at a glance","p":"Chapter 1: Introduction","u":"introduction.html#roles-at-a-glance","x":"Every staff user has one of three roles. The role decides which menus they see and what they can do. The table shows the main areas; each chapter gives the details. Area Admin Operations Finance Requests and quotes Yes Yes — Trips (create, move, reassign, cancel) Yes Yes View a trip only Trip value and profit Yes — Yes Trip expenses Yes — Yes Trucks, drivers and locations Yes Yes — Service types Yes — — Clients and portal users Yes — Yes Invoices, proformas and payments Yes — Yes Accounts and transfers Yes — Yes Reports Yes — Yes Users, settings, expense categories, audit log Yes — — Note: Operations users never see trip values, expenses or profit. This is enforced by the system, not just hidden on screen."},{"t":"Getting Started","p":"Chapter 2","u":"getting-started.html","x":""},{"t":"Logging in","p":"Chapter 2: Getting Started","u":"getting-started.html#logging-in","x":"Open the system’s web address in your browser. Enter the Username and Password your administrator gave you. Click “Log in.” The first time you log in, and after an administrator resets your password, the system asks you to choose a new password before you can do anything else. A password needs at least 8 characters, with upper and lower case letters and at least one number. Note: 5 incorrect password attempts lock the login for 15 minutes. The message tells you how many attempts you have left. If your account has been deactivated, you can’t log in at all: contact your administrator. There is no “Forgot password” link. Only an administrator can reset your password, from the Users page (see section 13.1)."},{"t":"Staying signed in","p":"Chapter 2: Getting Started","u":"getting-started.html#staying-signed-in","x":"After 30 minutes without activity, the system signs you out. Save your work before you step away. Each account can be signed in in one place at a time. Signing in on another computer or browser signs you out of the first one. Log out from the menu under your name at the top right when you finish."},{"t":"Finding your way around","p":"Chapter 2: Getting Started","u":"getting-started.html#finding-your-way-around","x":"The menu down the left side is how you get around. What you see in it depends on your role. Menu section What’s inside Who sees it Dashboard An overview for your role (see section 2.4) Everyone Operations Requests, Trips, Trucks, Drivers, Locations, and Service Types (Admin only) Admin, Operations Finance Clients, Invoices, Proformas, Payments, Accounts Admin, Finance Reports All reports Admin, Finance Administration Expense Categories, Users, Settings, Audit Log Admin The arrows at the bottom of the menu shrink it to icons, to give pages more room. On a phone, open the menu with the three-line button at the top left. The top bar shows the page title, the buttons for that page, a light and dark theme switch, and your name. Click your name for “Profile” and “Log out.” Most lists have filters at the top, such as status, client or date. Choose what you want and click “Filter.”"},{"t":"The Dashboard","p":"Chapter 2: Getting Started","u":"getting-started.html#the-dashboard","x":"The Dashboard shows a different overview for each role: Role What the Dashboard shows Admin Revenue This Month, Outstanding Receivables, Active Trips, Pending Quotes, Fleet Status, Recent Payments, and Overdue Invoices Operations Trucks Available, Drivers Available, Pending Requests, Quoted Requests, Active Trips, Trips Arriving Today, and Driver License Expiry Alerts Finance Outstanding Receivables, Active Trips, Expenses This Month, Receivables Aging, Recent Payments, and Expenses by Category for this month Note: Driver License Expiry Alerts list drivers whose licence has expired or expires within the next 30 days."},{"t":"Your profile and password","p":"Chapter 2: Getting Started","u":"getting-started.html#your-profile-and-password","x":"Click your name at the top right, then “Profile.” You can change your full name, email and phone, then click “Save Changes.” To change your password, enter your current password and the new one twice, then click “Update Password.” Note: Only an administrator can change your username or your role."},{"t":"Transport Requests","p":"Chapter 3","u":"transport-requests.html","x":"A transport request is a client asking you to move something: what, from where, to where, and when. Requests come in two ways: staff record them (source “Internal”), or clients submit them in the client portal (source “Portal”). Requests are under Operations → Requests. Who can do this: Admin and Operations."},{"t":"How a request moves","p":"Chapter 3: Transport Requests","u":"transport-requests.html#how-a-request-moves","x":"Status What it means What can happen next Pending New, with no quote yet Add a quote, edit, reject, or delete Quoted At least one quote has been sent Revise the quote, accept, or reject Accepted The client agreed to the latest quote Convert it to a trip Rejected Turned down, with a reason Reopen it (back to Pending), or delete it Converted A trip has been created from it Nothing; follow the trip instead The “Timeline” on the request page records every change of status, who made it, when, and any comment."},{"t":"Recording a request","p":"Chapter 3: Transport Requests","u":"transport-requests.html#recording-a-request","x":"Operations → Requests → “New Request.” Choose the Client. Optionally choose a Service Type. Choose the Origin and the Destination. They must be different places. Enter the Cargo Description, and optionally the Weight (tons), the Preferred Pickup Date and any Special Instructions. If you already know the price, tick “Add a quote now” and fill in the quote (see section 3.3). Save. The request gets a number such as REQ-2026-0001 and starts as Pending, or Quoted if you added a quote. Note: If a location or service type is missing from the list, click “+ Add” next to the field to create it without leaving the form. Only an Admin can add service types."},{"t":"Quoting","p":"Chapter 3: Transport Requests","u":"transport-requests.html#quoting","x":"On the request page, click “+ Add Quote” (or “+ Revise Quote” once there is one). Enter the Amount, the Currency, the date the quote is Valid Until (today or later), and any Notes, then click “Save Quote.” The first quote moves the request from Pending to Quoted. You can revise a quote as often as you need while the request is Pending or Quoted. Each revision gets its own number (QUO-…) and the newest is marked “Latest.” The client sees the latest one. When an Admin creates a trip from the request, the latest quote’s amount and currency are filled in as the trip’s value (see section 4.1)."},{"t":"Accepting or rejecting","p":"Chapter 3: Transport Requests","u":"transport-requests.html#accepting-or-rejecting","x":"When the client agrees, click “Accept” on the request page. Clients can also accept or reject a quote themselves in the portal (see section 14.2); the timeline then shows “Client (portal)” as the person who made the change. To turn a Pending or Quoted request down, click “Reject” and enter the reason. A rejected request can be brought back with “Reopen” and a comment; it returns to Pending."},{"t":"Editing and deleting","p":"Chapter 3: Transport Requests","u":"transport-requests.html#editing-and-deleting","x":"Only a Pending request can be edited. Once it is quoted, change the price with a new quote revision; once accepted, the request is locked. Only a Pending or Rejected request can be deleted."},{"t":"Finding a request","p":"Chapter 3: Transport Requests","u":"transport-requests.html#finding-a-request","x":"The Requests list can be filtered by status, source (Internal or Portal), client and service type. Click “View” to open a request."},{"t":"Trips","p":"Chapter 4","u":"trips.html","x":"A trip is the actual job: a truck and a driver carrying a client’s cargo from one place to another. Trips are under Operations → Trips. Who can do this: Admin and Operations create and manage trips. Finance can open a trip (for example from an invoice or a client) to see its value and expenses and to log expenses, but can’t change the trip itself."},{"t":"Creating a trip","p":"Chapter 4: Trips","u":"trips.html#creating-a-trip","x":"Most trips start from an accepted request: Open the accepted request and click “Convert to Trip.” The New Trip form opens with the request’s details filled in. Choose the Truck and the Driver. Check the Client, Origin, Destination (they must differ), Cargo Description and Weight (tons), and set the Pickup Time if you know it. Admins also see Trip Value, Currency and Exchange Rate, filled in from the latest quote. Check them. Click “Create Trip.” The trip gets a number such as TRP-2026-0001 and starts as Assigned. The request becomes Converted. You can also create a trip without a request: Operations → Trips → “New Trip.” Note: A truck or driver who is already on another active trip can’t be assigned. When a trip is created, its truck and driver change to “On Trip” automatically. Caution: Only an Admin can enter a trip’s value. A trip created by an Operations user is saved without one, even when it comes from a quoted request. An Admin should open the trip, click “Edit” and enter the Trip Value before it is invoiced; otherwise the invoice line starts at zero and the trip shows as a loss in the reports."},{"t":"Moving a trip through its stages","p":"Chapter 4: Trips","u":"trips.html#moving-a-trip-through-its-stages","x":"On the trip page, the button for the next stage moves the trip forward. You can add an optional comment each time, and the system records the time. From Button To Also recorded Assigned “Start Loading” Loading — Loading “Depart (En Route)” En Route Departure time En Route “Mark Delivered” Delivered Arrival time Delivered “Complete Trip” Completed Completion time Delivered “Start Return Journey” Returning Return departure time Returning “Complete Trip” Completed Return arrival and completion time When a trip is completed, its truck and driver go back to Available. Stages can’t be skipped or undone, so check before you click."},{"t":"The trip page","p":"Chapter 4: Trips","u":"trips.html#the-trip-page","x":"The trip page shows the truck, driver, route, cargo and the recorded times, with four tabs: Overview: the trip details. Admin and Finance also see the Financial Summary: Trip Value, Total Expenses, Profit / Loss, and Invoice Status, with a “Create Invoice” link if the trip hasn’t been invoiced yet. Expenses: the expenses logged against the trip (Admin and Finance only). Timeline: every change of stage, with who made it, when, and any comment. Assignment History: every change of truck or driver."},{"t":"Reassigning a truck or driver","p":"Chapter 4: Trips","u":"trips.html#reassigning-a-truck-or-driver","x":"If a truck breaks down or a driver can’t continue, click “Reassign” on an active trip. Choose the new truck and/or driver, and decide for each whether the one you’re replacing should be “Set back to available.” Untick it if, say, the old truck is going for repair. Add a note and save. The change is recorded in the Assignment History."},{"t":"Logging trip expenses","p":"Chapter 4: Trips","u":"trips.html#logging-trip-expenses","x":"Who can do this: Admin and Finance. Open the trip and go to the Expenses tab. Click “+ Log Expense.” Choose the Account the money came from and the expense Category (e.g. Fuel, Toll, Driver Allowance). Enter the Amount, the Currency and, for a foreign currency, the Exchange Rate to cedis. Add a Description, the date, and attach a photo or scan of the Receipt if you have one. Save. The amount is taken off the account’s balance straight away. To take an expense off, click “Remove.” The amount goes back into the account, and the reversal shows in the account’s ledger. Note: A trip’s Profit / Loss is its value (converted to cedis) minus its expenses. Log every expense against the right trip to keep the Trip Profitability report accurate."},{"t":"Editing a trip and its value","p":"Chapter 4: Trips","u":"trips.html#editing-a-trip-and-its-value","x":"Click “Edit” on the trip page to correct its details. Only an Admin sees and changes the trip value, currency and exchange rate. Once the trip has departed (En Route and later), the Audit Log records any change to them as a financial override."},{"t":"Cancelling or deleting a trip","p":"Chapter 4: Trips","u":"trips.html#cancelling-or-deleting-a-trip","x":"To cancel a trip that hasn’t been completed, click “Cancel Trip” and enter the reason. Its truck and driver go back to Available. If expenses have been logged against the trip, it can’t be cancelled until an Admin ticks “Override and cancel even if expenses have been logged.” Check the Expenses tab first. A trip can only be deleted if it has no logged expenses and isn’t on any invoice. Otherwise cancel it. Caution: Cancelling a trip can’t be undone."},{"t":"Fleet: Trucks and Drivers","p":"Chapter 5","u":"fleet-trucks-and-drivers.html","x":"Who can do this: Admin and Operations, under Operations → Trucks and Operations → Drivers."},{"t":"Trucks","p":"Chapter 5: Fleet: Trucks and Drivers","u":"fleet-trucks-and-drivers.html#trucks","x":"Click “New Truck” and enter the Plate Number, Truck Type, Make, Model, Year, Capacity (tons) and any Notes. Status Meaning How it changes Available Free for a new trip Automatically when a trip ends, or with “Set Available” On Trip Assigned to an active trip Automatically; you can’t change it by hand Maintenance Off the road for repair or service “Set Maintenance” Inactive No longer in use “Deactivate”; “Activate” brings it back as Available A truck on a trip can’t be put into maintenance or deactivated. An inactive truck must be activated before its maintenance status can change. A truck can only be deleted if it has never been assigned to a trip. Otherwise deactivate it, which keeps its history."},{"t":"Drivers","p":"Chapter 5: Fleet: Trucks and Drivers","u":"fleet-trucks-and-drivers.html#drivers","x":"Click “New Driver” and enter the Full Name, Phone, License Number, License Expiry and any Notes. Drivers have the same kinds of status as trucks: Available, On Trip, On Leave (“Set On Leave” and “Set Available”), and Inactive. The same rules apply: a driver on a trip can’t be put on leave or deactivated, and a driver who has ever been on a trip can’t be deleted. Note: The Drivers list highlights a licence that has expired or expires within 30 days, and the Operations dashboard lists them too. Renew the licence and update the expiry date on the driver’s record."},{"t":"Locations and Service Types","p":"Chapter 6","u":"locations-and-service-types.html","x":""},{"t":"Locations","p":"Chapter 6: Locations and Service Types","u":"locations-and-service-types.html#locations","x":"Who can do this: Admin and Operations, under Operations → Locations. Locations are the pickup and delivery points used on requests and trips. Each has a Name, City, Region and Country (Ghana unless you change it). Add them here with “New Location,” or on the fly with “+ Add” on a request form. A location that is used on a request or trip can’t be deleted; deactivate it instead so it no longer appears in the lists."},{"t":"Service types","p":"Chapter 6: Locations and Service Types","u":"locations-and-service-types.html#service-types","x":"Who can do this: Admin only, under Operations → Service Types. Service types describe the kinds of work you do, for example general haulage or container transport. Each has a Name, an optional Description and an optional Default Rate/Ton. Choosing a service type on a request is optional, but it lets the Revenue report break your income down by service. A service type that is in use can’t be deleted; deactivate it instead."},{"t":"Clients","p":"Chapter 7","u":"clients.html","x":"Who can do this: Admin and Finance, under Finance → Clients."},{"t":"Adding a client","p":"Chapter 7: Clients","u":"clients.html#adding-a-client","x":"Finance → Clients → “New Client.” Enter the Company Name, Contact Person, Email (each client needs its own) and Phone. Optionally add the Address, City, Region, Country, Default Currency and Notes. Save. The client gets a code such as CLT-2026-0001."},{"t":"The client page","p":"Chapter 7: Clients","u":"clients.html#the-client-page","x":"Open a client to see everything about them, on tabs: Overview: company details, Lifetime Revenue (all payments received) and Outstanding Balance (what is still owed on issued invoices). Portal Users: the client’s logins for the client portal (see section 7.3). Requests, Trips, Invoices & Balances, Payments: the client’s most recent records of each kind. Statement: every invoice and payment in date order, with a running balance."},{"t":"Portal users","p":"Chapter 7: Clients","u":"clients.html#portal-users","x":"Portal users are people at the client company who can sign in to the client portal (Chapter 14). A client can have several. Open the client and go to the Portal Users tab. Click “Add Portal User.” Enter their Full Name, Email and a Password (twice), then click “Create Portal User.” Give them the portal address and their password. They sign in with their email address. “Reset Password” creates a new password and shows it once in a message at the top of the page. Copy it straight away and share it securely: it can’t be shown again. Resetting also signs the user out of the portal. “Deactivate” stops a portal user from signing in, for example when they leave the company. “Activate” lets them back in."},{"t":"Deactivating or deleting a client","p":"Chapter 7: Clients","u":"clients.html#deactivating-or-deleting-a-client","x":"A client with trips, invoices or payments can’t be deleted; the message lists what is in the way. Deactivate the client instead: they stop appearing as an active client, and their history stays."},{"t":"Invoices","p":"Chapter 8","u":"invoices.html","x":"Who can do this: Admin and Finance, under Finance → Invoices."},{"t":"How an invoice moves","p":"Chapter 8: Invoices","u":"invoices.html#how-an-invoice-moves","x":"Status What it means Draft Being prepared. Fully editable, and not visible to the client. Issued Sent to the client and visible in their portal. Only the due date and notes can still change. Partially Paid Some payment received (set automatically) Paid Paid in full (set automatically) Cancelled Withdrawn, with a reason An invoice is overdue when it is Issued or Partially Paid and its due date has passed. Tick “Overdue only” on the Invoices list to see them; Admins also see them on the Dashboard."},{"t":"Creating an invoice","p":"Chapter 8: Invoices","u":"invoices.html#creating-an-invoice","x":"Finance → Invoices → “New Invoice.” (Or click “Create Invoice” on a trip, which starts the invoice with that trip.) Step 1: Client. Choose the client and click “Next: Select Trips.” Step 2: Select Trips. Tick the client’s trips to bill. Only trips that aren’t already on another invoice are listed. Then click “Next: Edit Line Items.” Step 3: Line Items. Each trip becomes a line using its trip value. Edit the descriptions, quantities and prices if needed, and use “+ Add Manual Line” for anything else, such as demurrage. Set the Invoice Date, the Due Date (on or after the invoice date) and the Tax Rate (%) if you charge tax. Add any Notes. Decide whether to tick “Show itemized trip expenses on PDF” (see section 8.4). Click “Create Invoice (Draft).” The invoice gets a number such as INV-2026-0001. Subtotal, tax and total are worked out for you. The invoice is in the client’s currency, taken from the trips you add."},{"t":"Issuing, editing and cancelling","p":"Chapter 8: Invoices","u":"invoices.html#issuing-editing-and-cancelling","x":"Check a draft, then click “Issue Invoice.” The client can now see it in the portal and you can record payments against it. Click “Edit” to change a draft completely, or to change an issued invoice’s due date and notes. To withdraw an invoice, click “Cancel” and enter the reason. An invoice with payments recorded can’t be cancelled. Only a draft can be deleted. Cancel an issued invoice instead. Note: Once an invoice is cancelled, its trips are free to be billed on a new invoice."},{"t":"Invoice PDFs","p":"Chapter 8: Invoices","u":"invoices.html#invoice-pdfs","x":"The “PDF” button on an invoice offers two versions: Itemized PDF: lists each trip’s logged expenses under its line. Use it when a client has agreed to pay costs such as fuel or tolls at cost. Clean PDF: just the lines, prices and totals. Clients downloading an invoice in the portal always get the clean version, so internal costs are never shown to them. The PDF uses your organization’s details, logo and invoice footer notes from Settings (section 13.2)."},{"t":"Payments","p":"Chapter 9","u":"payments.html","x":"Who can do this: Admin and Finance."},{"t":"Recording a payment","p":"Chapter 9: Payments","u":"payments.html#recording-a-payment","x":"Open the invoice. It must be Issued or Partially Paid. Click “+ Record Payment.” The form shows what is still outstanding. Choose the Account the money went into. Enter the Amount, the Currency and, for a foreign currency, the Exchange Rate to cedis. Choose the Payment Method (Cash, Bank Transfer, Mobile Money, Cheque or Other), and enter the Reference (e.g. a transaction ID), the payment date and any notes. Click “Save Payment.” The payment gets a number such as PAY-2026-0001. The account’s balance goes up straight away, and the invoice changes to Partially Paid or Paid by itself. Note: If a payment is more than the outstanding balance, the form warns you and asks you to confirm before saving the overpayment."},{"t":"The Payments list","p":"Chapter 9: Payments","u":"payments.html#the-payments-list","x":"Finance → Payments lists every payment, with filters for client, payment method and account. Click an invoice number to open the invoice."},{"t":"Deleting a payment","p":"Chapter 9: Payments","u":"payments.html#deleting-a-payment","x":"On the invoice page, click “Delete” next to the payment. The amount is taken back off the account, the reversal shows in the account’s ledger, and the invoice’s status is worked out again. Caution: Delete a payment only if it was recorded by mistake. To correct one, delete it and record it again with the right details."},{"t":"Proforma Invoices","p":"Chapter 10","u":"proforma-invoices.html","x":"A proforma invoice is a priced document you send a client before the work is done, for example so they can raise a purchase order. It has its own numbering (PFI-2026-0001), so it never uses up an invoice number. Proformas are under Finance → Proformas. Who can do this: Admin and Finance."},{"t":"Turning it on","p":"Chapter 10: Proforma Invoices","u":"proforma-invoices.html#turning-it-on","x":"Proformas are switched on by default. An Admin can switch them off, and set the default validity and the PDF footer, under Administration → Settings → Proforma Invoices (see section 13.2). When they are off, Proformas disappears from the staff menu and from the client portal."},{"t":"Creating a proforma","p":"Chapter 10: Proforma Invoices","u":"proforma-invoices.html#creating-a-proforma","x":"Finance → Proformas → “New Proforma,” or “+ Create Proforma” on a request page. Choose the Client. Choose “Start from a quoted request” to link it to one of the client’s requests and copy in its quote, or “Start without a request.” Add the line items: Description, Qty and Unit Price for each, with “+ Add Line” for more. Set the Issue Date, Valid Until (filled in from the default validity in Settings), Currency, Tax Rate (%) and any Notes. Save. The proforma starts as a draft."},{"t":"Issuing it","p":"Chapter 10: Proforma Invoices","u":"proforma-invoices.html#issuing-it","x":"Check the draft, then click “Issue Proforma.” Its prices are locked, and the client can see and download it in the portal. Click “Download PDF” for a copy to send by email."},{"t":"Editing, expiry and cancelling","p":"Chapter 10: Proforma Invoices","u":"proforma-invoices.html#editing-expiry-and-cancelling","x":"A draft can be edited completely, or deleted. Once issued, only the Valid Until date and the notes can change. An issued proforma whose Valid Until date has passed shows as Expired. The client’s portal tells them to contact you for an updated one. Extend the date if the price still stands. To withdraw a proforma, click “Cancel” and enter the reason. The client’s portal shows it as withdrawn and no longer valid."},{"t":"Converting it to an invoice","p":"Chapter 10: Proforma Invoices","u":"proforma-invoices.html#converting-it-to-an-invoice","x":"When the client goes ahead, open the issued proforma and click “Convert to Invoice.” The system creates a draft invoice with the same lines, currency and tax, dated today and due in 30 days, with a note saying which proforma it came from. The proforma becomes Converted, and the invoice shows “Converted From” with a link back. Review the draft invoice, attach the trips if you like, then issue it as usual (section 8.3). Note: If that invoice is later cancelled or deleted, the proforma goes back to Issued, so it can be converted again."},{"t":"Accounts and Cash Book","p":"Chapter 11","u":"accounts-and-cash-book.html","x":"Who can do this: Admin and Finance, under Finance → Accounts."},{"t":"Cash, mobile money and bank accounts","p":"Chapter 11: Accounts and Cash Book","u":"accounts-and-cash-book.html#cash-mobile-money-and-bank-accounts","x":"Accounts are where your money is: office cash, mobile money wallets and bank accounts. Every payment received, expense paid, transfer and bank charge moves an account’s balance, and each account keeps a full ledger. All balances are kept in cedis (GHS). A new system starts with three accounts, Office Cash, MTN Mobile Money and Main Bank Account, all with a zero balance. Edit them to match your real accounts before you start. The Accounts page shows each account with its balance, and the Total Balance of all active accounts. One account of each type can be the default, which is picked first on forms."},{"t":"Adding an account","p":"Chapter 11: Accounts and Cash Book","u":"accounts-and-cash-book.html#adding-an-account","x":"Finance → Accounts → “New Account.” Enter the Account Name and choose the Type: Cash, Mobile Money or Bank. For Mobile Money, choose the Network (MTN, Telecel or AirtelTigo) and enter the Number and Registered Name. For Bank, enter the Bank Name and Account Number, and optionally the Branch, Account Name and SWIFT Code. Enter the Opening Balance (GHS): what the account holds today. Tick “Set as default for this type” if it should be picked first on forms. Click “Create Account.” Note: After an account is created, you can change its name, notes, default and active status, but not its type or opening balance."},{"t":"The account page and ledger","p":"Chapter 11: Accounts and Cash Book","u":"accounts-and-cash-book.html#the-account-page-and-ledger","x":"Open an account to see its Current Balance; This Month In, Out and Net; and the Transaction Ledger: every credit and debit, with the balance after each one."},{"t":"Moving money between accounts","p":"Chapter 11: Accounts and Cash Book","u":"accounts-and-cash-book.html#moving-money-between-accounts","x":"Finance → Accounts → “Transfer.” Choose the From Account and the To Account. The form shows each one’s balance. Enter the Amount (GHS), the Date and a Description. If the transfer cost a fee, open “Transfer Charges (optional),” choose the Charge Account the fee came out of, and enter the Transfer Charge (GHS), and any Other Charges (GHS), with descriptions. Click “Execute Transfer.” It gets a reference such as TRF-2026-0001."},{"t":"Recording a bank charge","p":"Chapter 11: Accounts and Cash Book","u":"accounts-and-cash-book.html#recording-a-bank-charge","x":"For a charge that isn’t part of a transfer, such as a monthly bank fee, open the account and click “+ Record Bank Charge.” Enter the Description, Amount (GHS) and Date, then click “Save Charge.”"},{"t":"Deactivating or deleting an account","p":"Chapter 11: Accounts and Cash Book","u":"accounts-and-cash-book.html#deactivating-or-deleting-an-account","x":"An account can only be deleted if its balance is zero and it has no transactions. Otherwise, deactivate it from its Edit page: it stops appearing on forms and its history stays."},{"t":"Reports","p":"Chapter 12","u":"reports.html","x":"Who can do this: Admin and Finance, under Reports."},{"t":"The reports","p":"Chapter 12: Reports","u":"reports.html#the-reports","x":"Report What it shows Revenue Invoiced revenue by client, by service type and by month Receivables Aging What each client owes, grouped by how long it is overdue: 0-30, 31-60, 61-90 and 90+ days, with the invoices behind each figure Trip Profitability Each trip’s value minus its expenses, grouped by client, by truck and by month Expenses Expenses by category, by truck and by trip Payments Payments collected by method, by client and by account Fleet Utilization Trips per truck, with active and idle days Client Statement One client’s invoices and payments with a running balance Most reports have “From” and “To” dates. Set them and click “Filter.” “Export CSV” downloads the figures to open in Excel. “Print” prints the page. All amounts are in cedis (GHS)."},{"t":"Client statements","p":"Chapter 12: Reports","u":"reports.html#client-statements","x":"Reports → Client Statement: choose the client, set the From and To dates, and click “Generate.” Use the PDF button to download a statement to send to the client."},{"t":"Administration","p":"Chapter 13","u":"administration.html","x":"Who can do this: Admin only, under Administration."},{"t":"Users","p":"Chapter 13: Administration","u":"administration.html#users","x":""},{"t":"Creating a user","p":"Chapter 13: Administration › Users","u":"administration.html#users-creating-a-user","x":"Administration → Users → “New User.” Enter the Full Name, Username, Email and optionally the Phone. Choose the Role: Admin, Finance or Operations (see section 1.5). Enter a starting Password twice: at least 8 characters, with upper and lower case letters and a number. Save, and give the user their username and password. They must choose a new password when they first log in."},{"t":"Managing an existing user","p":"Chapter 13: Administration › Users","u":"administration.html#users-managing-an-existing-user","x":"“Edit” changes their details or role. “Reset Password” creates a new password and shows it once in a message at the top of the page. Copy it and give it to the user securely. They must change it at their next login, and any session they had open is ended. “Deactivate” stops a user logging in and signs them out. You can’t deactivate your own account. “Activate” lets them back in."},{"t":"Settings","p":"Chapter 13: Administration","u":"administration.html#settings","x":"Administration → Settings holds the details printed on your documents and a few switches: Section Settings Organization Details Organization Name, Phone, Email, Address, Default Currency, and Logo (with “Remove current logo”) Invoice PDF “Default to itemized expense breakdown on invoice PDFs,” and Footer Notes printed at the bottom of every invoice Proforma Invoices “Enable proforma invoices,” Default Validity (days), and Proforma Footer Notes Click “Save Settings” when you’re done."},{"t":"Expense categories","p":"Chapter 13: Administration","u":"administration.html#expense-categories","x":"Expense categories group trip expenses for the reports. The system comes with nine: Fuel, Toll, Meal, Repair, Loading/Offloading, Tyre, Driver Allowance, Ferry/Crossing and Other. These built-in categories can’t be deleted, but you can deactivate any you don’t use. Add your own with “New Category.” A category you added can be deleted only if no expense uses it; otherwise deactivate it."},{"t":"Audit log","p":"Chapter 13: Administration","u":"administration.html#audit-log","x":"The Audit Log records every important change: what was created, changed, deleted, issued, cancelled or reassigned, by whom, when, and from which IP address. Click “Details” on an entry to see the values before and after the change. Filter by entity (e.g. Trip or Invoice), action, staff user, or user type (Staff or Client, for changes made in the portal)."},{"t":"The Client Portal","p":"Chapter 14","u":"the-client-portal.html","x":"The client portal is a separate area where your clients sign in to work with you directly. This chapter describes what they see, so you can help them."},{"t":"Signing in","p":"Chapter 14: The Client Portal","u":"the-client-portal.html#signing-in","x":"Clients go to the portal’s login page: your system’s address followed by /portal/login. They sign in with their email address and the password you set or reset for them (section 7.3). After 5 wrong passwords the login is locked for 15 minutes. After 15 minutes without activity they are signed out. A deactivated portal user sees a message telling them to contact their account manager."},{"t":"What clients can do","p":"Chapter 14: The Client Portal","u":"the-client-portal.html#what-clients-can-do","x":"Page What the client can do Dashboard See their active trips, their open invoice balance, and quotes awaiting their action My Requests Submit a new transport request, see your quotes, and accept or reject the latest one (with an optional comment) My Trips Follow each trip’s status and timeline My Invoices See issued invoices with their payments and balance, and download the PDF Proforma Invoices See issued proformas and download the PDF (only if proformas are switched on) Profile Update their details and change their password Note: Clients never see draft invoices or proformas, trip expenses, or anything about other clients. Requests they submit arrive in your Requests list as Pending, with the source “Portal.”"},{"t":"Frequently Asked Questions","p":"Appendix A","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html","x":""},{"t":"I forgot my password. What do I do?","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#i-forgot-my-password-what-do-i-do","x":"Ask an Admin to reset it from Administration → Users. You’ll get a new password and must choose your own when you log in."},{"t":"My login is locked. Why?","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#my-login-is-locked-why","x":"After 5 wrong passwords the login is locked for 15 minutes. Wait and try again, or ask an Admin to reset your password."},{"t":"I was signed out while working.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#i-was-signed-out-while-working","x":"The system signs you out after 30 minutes without activity, or when your account signs in somewhere else."},{"t":"A menu item I expect to see is missing.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#a-menu-item-i-expect-to-see-is-missing","x":"Menus depend on your role (section 1.5). Proformas also disappear when they are switched off in Settings."},{"t":"Why can’t I see the trip value or profit?","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#why-cant-i-see-the-trip-value-or-profit","x":"Operations users don’t see trip values, expenses or profit. Ask an Admin or Finance user."},{"t":"A truck or driver isn’t available when I create a trip.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#a-truck-or-driver-isnt-available-when-i-create-a-trip","x":"They are probably on another active trip, in maintenance or on leave, or deactivated. Check their status under Operations → Trucks or Drivers."},{"t":"I can’t put a truck into maintenance.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#i-cant-put-a-truck-into-maintenance","x":"A truck on a trip has its status managed automatically. Complete, cancel or reassign the trip first."},{"t":"I can’t cancel a trip.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#i-cant-cancel-a-trip","x":"Expenses have been logged against it. Check the Expenses tab; an Admin can override and cancel anyway."},{"t":"I can’t delete a trip.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#i-cant-delete-a-trip","x":"It has expenses or is on an invoice. Cancel it instead."},{"t":"A trip doesn’t appear when I create an invoice.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#a-trip-doesnt-appear-when-i-create-an-invoice","x":"It is already on another invoice that hasn’t been cancelled, or it belongs to a different client."},{"t":"I can’t cancel an invoice.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#i-cant-cancel-an-invoice","x":"It has payments recorded against it. If those payments were mistakes, delete them first (section 9.3)."},{"t":"My client can’t see their invoice in the portal.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#my-client-cant-see-their-invoice-in-the-portal","x":"It is still a draft. Issue it (section 8.3)."},{"t":"Proformas is missing from the Finance menu.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#proformas-is-missing-from-the-finance-menu","x":"An Admin has switched proformas off under Administration → Settings → Proforma Invoices."},{"t":"I can’t delete an account, client, truck or driver.","p":"Appendix A: Frequently Asked Questions","u":"frequently-asked-questions.html#i-cant-delete-an-account-client-truck-or-driver","x":"It has history the system must keep. Deactivate it instead; it stops appearing in lists and on forms."},{"t":"Document Information","p":"Appendix B","u":"document-information.html","x":"This manual was produced from the current state of the IBS Haulage codebase and reflects the screens, fields and roles in the application as of the date below. If the application is updated after this date, some field names or workflows described here may change. Version 1.0 — October 2026."}];
